      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's focus on the clinical signs of sepsis or peritonitis in your heifer post-rumenotomy. It's crucial to monitor her closely for any signs of infection or complications.\n\nFor sepsis, watch for:\n1. Fever: A rectal temperature above 39.2°C (102.5°F) is a red flag.\n2. Lethargy: Your heifer may seem unusually tired or uninterested in her surroundings.\n3. Decreased milk production: If she's a lactating cow, a drop in milk yield can indicate systemic infection.\n4. Rapid heart rate: Tachycardia, or a heart rate above 60 beats per minute, can be a sign of sepsis.\n5. Decreased appetite: A lack of interest in food can be a warning sign.\n\nFor peritonitis, look out for:\n1. Abdominal pain: Your heifer may show signs of discomfort, such as rolling or arching her back.\n2. Distended abdomen: Swelling or bloating in the belly area can indicate fluid accumulation.\n3. Decreased rumen motility: A lack of rumen contractions can be a sign of peritonitis.\n4. Shock: Signs of",
